<solution>
The patch addresses the vulnerability through two complementary changes that restore compliance with the security specifications:

**Change 1: Zero-Length Segment Handling in Iterator Advancement**
<enforcement spec="HS-INPUT-001">
<location>mm/filemap.c, __iov_iter_advance_iov()</location>
<diff>
	while (bytes) {
+	while (bytes || !iov->iov_len) {
		int copy = min(bytes, iov->iov_len - base);

		bytes -= copy;
		base += copy;
		if (iov->iov_len == base) {
			iov++;
			base = 0;
		}
	}
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix modifies the while loop condition from `while (bytes)` to `while (bytes || !iov->iov_len)`. This ensures the loop executes even when `bytes = 0` if the current iovec has zero length (`iov->iov_len == 0`). When this happens, `copy = min(0, 0 - base) = 0`, so `bytes` remains 0 and `base` remains unchanged. The check `if (iov->iov_len == base)` will be true (since both are 0), causing `iov++` and `base = 0`, effectively skipping the zero-length segment. This enforces HS-INPUT-001 by validating and skipping zero-length segments during advancement.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

**Change 2: Guaranteed Forward Progress in Error Handling**
<enforcement spec="HS-STATE-002">
<location>mm/filemap.c, generic_perform_write()</location>
<diff>
		cond_resched();

-		if (unlikely(copied == 0)) {
+		iov_iter_advance(i, copied);
+		if (unlikely(copied == 0)) {
			/*
			 * If we were unable to copy any data at all, we must
			 * fall back to a single segment length write.
			 *
			 * If we didn't fallback here, we could livelock
			 * because not all segments in the iov can be copied at
			 * once without a pagefault.
			 */
			bytes = min_t(unsigned long, PAGE_CACHE_SIZE - offset,
						iov_iter_single_seg_count(i));
			goto again;
		}
-		iov_iter_advance(i, copied);
		pos += copied;
		written += copied;
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix reorders the operations: `iov_iter_advance(i, copied)` is now called unconditionally before the `copied == 0` check. This guarantees forward progress through the iovec array regardless of whether any data was copied. When combined with Change 1, this ensures that even zero-length segments are properly skipped. The iterator state always advances, preventing the infinite loop. This enforces HS-STATE-002 by guaranteeing forward progress in iterator state transitions.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

**Security Impact**: The combined changes eliminate the denial-of-service vulnerability by:
1. Ensuring zero-length iovec segments are always skipped during iterator advancement
2. Guaranteeing forward progress through the iovec array even when no data is copied
3. Preventing the infinite loop condition that made processes unkillable

The fix is minimal and robust, addressing the root cause rather than just symptoms, and applies to all filesystem code using the iovec iterator interface.

