<location>src/squidclamav.c, replace() function</location>
<diff>
+char * /* <=== replace */ 
+replace(const char *s, const char *old, const char *new)
+{
+	char *ret;
+	int i, count = 0;
+	size_t newlen = strlen(new);
+	size_t oldlen = strlen(old);
+
+	for (i = 0; s[i] != '\0'; i++) {
+		if (strstr(&s[i], old) == &s[i]) {
+			count++;
+			i += oldlen - 1;
+		}
+	}
+	ret = malloc(i + 1 + count * (newlen - oldlen));
+	if (ret != NULL) {
+		i = 0;
+		while (*s) {
+			if (strstr(s, old) == s) {
+				strcpy(&ret[i], new);
+				i += newlen;
+				s += oldlen;
+			} else {
+				ret[i++] = *s++;
+			}
+		}
+		ret[i] = '\0';
+	}
+
+	return ret;
+}
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The new `replace()` function provides a safe string manipulation utility that properly handles substring replacement. By using this function to escape percent signs, the fix ensures consistent encoding semantics are maintained during data exchange between SquidClamav and squidGuard. The percent-encoded URL data is transformed to a canonical form where "%" becomes "%25", preventing any misinterpretation of the encoding across the protocol boundary.
