</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix inserts a taint validation loop immediately after retrieving the function interface structure but before any argument processing or memory allocation. It checks if the current safe level requires taint checking ($SAFE ≥ 1), then iterates through all arguments to verify none are tainted. If a tainted argument is found, it raises a SecurityError before any dangerous operations occur. This ensures compliance with HS-SEC-001 by validating taint status according to the current $SAFE level.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

**Change 2: Validation Before Type Conversion**
<enforcement spec="HS-INPUT-002">
<location>ext/fiddle/function.c, function_call()</location>
<diff>
    // The entire validation block is inserted BEFORE the argument processing loop
    TypedData_Get_Struct(self, ffi_cif, &function_data_type, cif);

+   if (rb_safe_level() >= 1) {
+       for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
+           VALUE src = argv[i];
+           if (OBJ_TAINTED(src)) {
+               rb_raise(rb_eSecurityError, "tainted parameter not allowed");
+           }
+       }
+   }
+   // ^ Validation occurs here, before any type conversion

    values = xcalloc((size_t)argc + 1, (size_t)sizeof(void *));
    generic_args = xcalloc((size_t)argc, (size_t)sizeof(fiddle_generic));

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        VALUE type = RARRAY_PTR(types)[i];
        VALUE src = argv[i];

        if(NUM2INT(type) == TYPE_VOIDP) {
            if(NIL_P(src)) {
                src = INT2FIX(0);
            } else if(cPointer != CLASS_OF(src)) {
                src = rb_funcall(cPointer, rb_intern("[]"), 1, src);
            }
            src = rb_Integer(src);  // Type conversion - now safe due to prior validation
        }

        VALUE2GENERIC(NUM2INT(type), src, &generic_args[i]);  // Marshaling - now safe
        values[i] = (void *)&generic_args[i];
    }
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        By placing the taint validation immediately after the argument count check and before any memory allocation or type conversion, the fix ensures that external data validation occurs before type conversion and marshaling operations. This prevents tainted data from influencing the `rb_Integer` conversion or the `VALUE2GENERIC` marshaling macro, both of which could have security implications if fed malicious input.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

**Additional Fix in DL Module:**
The same fix was applied to the legacy DL module in `ext/dl/lib/dl/func.rb` (Ruby code), showing consistent enforcement across both FFI interfaces:
```ruby
# In DL::Function#call method
if $SAFE >= 1 && args.any? {|arg| arg.tainted? }
  raise SecurityError, "tainted parameter not allowed"
end
```

**Security Impact:**
1. **Restores $SAFE Level Enforcement**: Native function calls now respect Ruby's security levels
2. **Prevents Sandbox Escape**: Tainted data cannot directly influence native code execution
3. **Defense in Depth**: Even if native libraries have vulnerabilities, attackers cannot feed them arbitrary tainted input
4. **Consistent Security Model**: Both Fiddle (C extension) and DL (Ruby) now enforce the same taint checking rules

