The fix moves modern-style negotiation from the root process to child processes, ensuring that negotiation failures only affect individual connections, not the entire server.

**Change 1: Isolate Modern Negotiation to Child Process**
<enforcement spec="HS-STATE-001">
<location>nbd-server.c, serveloop() and new handle_modern_connection() function</location>
<diff>
// Vulnerable code in serveloop():
for(i=0; i < modernsocks->len; i++) {
    int sock = g_array_index(modernsocks, int, i);
    if(!FD_ISSET(sock, &rset)) {
        continue;
    }
    CLIENT *client;

    if((net=accept(sock, (struct sockaddr *) &addrin, &addrinlen)) < 0) {
        err_nonfatal("accept: %m");
        continue;
    }
    client = negotiate(net, NULL, servers, NEG_INIT | NEG_MODERN); // IN ROOT PROCESS
    if(!client) {
        close(net);
        continue;
    }
    handle_connection(servers, net, client->server, client);
}

// Fixed code in serveloop():
for(i=0; i < modernsocks->len; i++) {
    int sock = g_array_index(modernsocks, int, i);
    if(!FD_ISSET(sock, &rset)) {
        continue;
    }

    handle_modern_connection(servers, sock); // Delegates to new function
}

// New handle_modern_connection() function:
static void handle_modern_connection(GArray *const servers, const int sock) {
    int net;
    pid_t pid;
    CLIENT *client = NULL;

    net = socket_accept(sock);
    if (net < 0)
        return;

    if (!dontfork) {
        pid = spawn_child(); // Fork BEFORE negotiation
        if (pid) {
            close(net);
            return;
        }
        // Child continues below
    }

    client = negotiate(net, NULL, servers, NEG_INIT | NEG_MODERN); // Now in child process
    if (!client) {
        msg(LOG_ERR, "Modern initial negotiation failed");
        goto handler_err;
    }
    // ... rest of connection handling in child process
}
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix creates a clear isolation boundary by moving the `negotiate()` call from the root process to a child process. The root process only accepts connections and forks children. All negotiation with untrusted clients occurs in isolated child processes, preventing client-induced errors from affecting the root server.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

**Change 2: Ensure Parent Resilience with Proper Fork Timing**
<enforcement spec="HS-TIMING-002">
<location>nbd-server.c, handle_modern_connection() and spawn_child() functions</location>
<diff>
// New spawn_child() function ensures proper signal handling:
static pid_t spawn_child() {
    pid_t pid;
    sigset_t newset;
    sigset_t oldset;

    sigemptyset(&newset);
    sigaddset(&newset, SIGCHLD);
    sigaddset(&newset, SIGTERM);
    sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK, &newset, &oldset); // Block signals during fork

    pid = fork();
    if (pid < 0) {
        msg(LOG_ERR, "Could not fork (%s)", strerror(errno));
        goto out;
    }

    if (pid > 0) { /* Parent */
        pid_t *pidp = g_malloc(sizeof(pid_t));
        *pidp = pid;
        g_hash_table_insert(children, pidp, pidp);
        goto out;
    }

    /* Child */
    signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_DFL);  // Reset signal handlers
    signal(SIGTERM, SIG_DFL);
    signal(SIGHUP, SIG_DFL);

out:
    sigprocmask(SIG_SETMASK, &oldset, NULL);
    return pid;
}

// In handle_modern_connection():
if (!dontfork) {
    pid = spawn_child();  // Fork happens BEFORE risky negotiation
    if (pid) {  // Parent
        close(net);
        return;  // Parent returns immediately, child continues
    }
    // Child process continues with negotiation
}
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix ensures the parent process remains resilient by:
        1. Forking child processes **before** performing risky negotiation operations
        2. Using proper signal blocking during fork to prevent race conditions
        3. Having the parent process immediately return to its main loop after forking
        4. Isolating all client interaction to the child process
        This guarantees that even if a child process crashes during negotiation, the parent (root server) continues running.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

