    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix adds early validation of the `requesting-user-name` attribute using `ippValidateAttribute()`. This function checks for valid UTF-8 encoding (among other validations) as required by the IPP specification. If validation fails, the job creation is rejected immediately with an appropriate error response, preventing the invalid string from propagating through the system.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

**Change 2: Protocol-Compliant Validation Order**
<enforcement spec="HS-PROTOCOL-002">
<location>scheduler/ipp.c, add_job()</location>
<diff>
  // The validation is moved to occur immediately after finding the attribute,
  // before any job object creation or downstream processing
  attr = ippFindAttribute(con->request, "requesting-user-name", IPP_TAG_NAME);

  if (attr && !ippValidateAttribute(attr))
  {
    send_ipp_status(con, IPP_ATTRIBUTES, _("Bad requesting-user-name value: %s"), cupsLastErrorString());
    if ((attr = ippCopyAttribute(con->response, attr, 0)) != NULL)
      attr->group_tag = IPP_TAG_UNSUPPORTED_GROUP;
    return (NULL);
  }

  // Only after validation passes does the function proceed to create the job
  if ((job = cupsdAddJob(priority, printer->name)) == NULL)
  {
    send_ipp_status(con, IPP_INTERNAL_ERROR,
                    _("Unable to add job for destination \"%s\"."),
		    printer->name);
    return (NULL);
  }
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix ensures IPP attribute validation occurs before any downstream system interaction. By validating `requesting-user-name` immediately after retrieving it and before creating the job object, the system prevents invalid data from reaching D-Bus or other subsystems. This follows the principle of failing fast and maintaining proper protocol validation ordering.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

