    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix adds a new, crucial check: `e = __lookup_free_nid_list(nm_i, nid);`. This function looks up the `nid` in the free nid radix tree *while holding the nid_list_lock*. This check is definitive:
        1.  If the `nid` is found in the `FREE_NID_LIST`, it is already tracked as free (or potentially stale), so it should not be added again.
        2.  If the `nid` is found in the `ALLOC_NID_LIST` (which also resides in the same `free_nid_root` radix tree but with `state = NID_ALLOC`), this check will also find it, correctly preventing its addition to the free list. This explicitly enforces exclusive ownership; a `nid` cannot be in the free list if it is already in any other managed state list (`ALLOC_NID_LIST`). The combined atomic check of NAT cache and free/alloc list guarantees a `nid` is truly free before transitioning it to the `FREE_NID_LIST` state.
