The patch fundamentally changes the strategy for handling stack references during garbage collection. It separates the concern of **marking live objects** from the concern of **sanitizing unused/dead references**, and does so in a safe order.

**Change 1: Eliminate Unsafe Dereference During Marking**
<enforcement spec="HS-MEM-003">
<location>src/gc.c, mark_context_stack()</location>
<diff>
  for (i=0; i<e; i++) {
    mrb_value v = c->stbase[i];

    if (!mrb_immediate_p(v)) {
-     if (mrb_basic_ptr(v)->tt == MRB_TT_FREE) {
-       c->stbase[i] = mrb_nil_value();
-     }
-     else {
-       mrb_gc_mark(mrb, mrb_basic_ptr(v));
-     }
+     mrb_gc_mark(mrb, mrb_basic_ptr(v));
    }
  }
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix removes the conditional check `if (mrb_basic_ptr(v)->tt == MRB_TT_FREE)` entirely. Now, for every non-immediate value on the stack, it unconditionally calls `mrb_gc_mark`. The `mrb_gc_mark` function contains a guard check (`if (!is_white(obj)) return;`) and importantly, an assertion (`mrb_assert((obj)->tt != MRB_TT_FREE);`). This moves the responsibility of handling invalid pointers to a centralized location. If a pointer is truly bad (points to a `MRB_TT_FREE` object), the assertion will fail in debugging modes, preventing silent corruption. In production, the `is_white` check may fail, causing the function to return early without dereferencing `obj`. This is safer than the original direct dereference.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

**Change 2: Proactive Sanitization of Entire Stack Region**
<enforcement spec="HS-STATE-002">
<location>src/gc.c, mark_context_stack()</location>
<diff>
+  e = c->stend - c->stbase;
+  nil = mrb_nil_value();
+  for (; i<e; i++) {
+    c->stbase[i] = nil;
+  }
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        After the marking loop completes, the variable `i` holds the index just past the last processed ("used") stack slot. The fix adds a second loop that continues from `i` to the absolute end of the stack (`c->stend`). It overwrites every single remaining slot in the stack's allocated memory with `nil`. This is a **proactive sanitization** of the entire reference container. It ensures that any memory region that could potentially hold a reference (including unused portions or slots that previously held now-freed objects) is wiped clean. This action is taken during every GC mark phase, guaranteeing that stale pointers cannot persist across GC cycles to cause issues later. It enforces the principle that the state of the reference container is explicitly managed and made safe.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

