The patch addresses the vulnerability by introducing proper global serialization between `lo_open()` and `lo_release()` operations.

**Change 1: Global Serialization of Release Operation**
<enforcement spec="HS-TIMING-001">
<location>drivers/block/loop.c, lo_release()</location>
<diff>
 static void lo_release(struct gendisk *disk, fmode_t mode)
 {
 	struct loop_device *lo = disk->private_data;
 	int err;

 	if (atomic_dec_return(&lo->lo_refcnt))
 		return;

 	mutex_lock(&lo->lo_ctl_mutex);
 	if (lo->lo_flags & LO_FLAGS_AUTOCLEAR) {
 		/*
 		 * In autoclear mode, stop the loop thread
 		 * and remove configuration after last close.
 		 */
 		err = loop_clr_fd(lo);
 		if (!err)
 			return;
 	} else if (lo->lo_state == Lo_bound) {
 		/*
 		 * Otherwise keep thread (if running) and config,
 		 * but flush possible ongoing bios in thread.
 		 */
 		blk_mq_freeze_queue(lo->lo_queue);
 		blk_mq_unfreeze_queue(lo->lo_queue);
 	}

 	mutex_unlock(&lo->lo_ctl_mutex);
 }
+
+static void lo_release(struct gendisk *disk, fmode_t mode)
+{
+	mutex_lock(&loop_index_mutex);
+	__lo_release(disk->private_data);
+	mutex_unlock(&loop_index_mutex);
+}
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fix restructures the code by splitting the release logic into two functions. The new `lo_release()` acquires the global `loop_index_mutex` before calling the actual release logic in `__lo_release()`. This ensures that release operations are serialized with open operations (which also use `loop_index_mutex`), preventing concurrent open/release races.
