<solution>
The patch addresses the vulnerability by adding proper validation during the greedy matching process to ensure pointer advancement doesn't exceed buffer boundaries.

**Change 1: Add Length Validation in Greedy Matching**
<enforcement spec="HS-INPUT-005">
<location>lib/ldb/ldb_match.c, ldb_wildcard_compare()</location>
<diff>
			do { /* greedy */
				uint8_t *haystack
					= p + cnk.length;
				size_t haystacklen
					= end - (haystack);

				g = memmem(haystack,
					   haystacklen,
					(const uint8_t *)cnk.data,
					cnk.length);
-				if (g) p = g;
+				if (g) {
+					p = g;
+				}
			} while(g);
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        While this change appears minor (just adding braces), it's part of a broader fix that ensures the greedy matching algorithm maintains consistent state. The critical fix is the validation that occurs after the loop, ensuring that pointer advancement doesn't cause length underflow.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

**Change 2: Prevent Length Underflow After Pointer Update**
<enforcement spec="HS-INPUT-005">
<location>lib/ldb/ldb_match.c, ldb_wildcard_compare()</location>
<diff>
		val.length = val.length - (p - (uint8_t *)(val.data)) - cnk.length;
		val.data = (uint8_t *)(p + cnk.length);
		c++;
		talloc_free(cnk.data);
		cnk.data = NULL;
	}

	/* last chunk may not have reached end of string */
-	if ( (! tree->u.substring.end_with_wildcard) && (*(val.data) != 0) ) goto mismatch;
+	if ( (! tree->u.substring.end_with_wildcard) && (val.length != 0) ) goto mismatch;
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        This is the critical fix that restores compliance with HS-INPUT-005. The change replaces the unsafe pointer dereference `*(val.data) != 0` with a length check `val.length != 0`. This ensures:
        1. No out-of-bounds read occurs (addressing HS-MEM-003)
        2. The algorithm uses the maintained length state rather than assuming null termination
        3. Even if `val.length` became inconsistent due to greedy matching, the check uses the actual maintained state

        The fix ensures that pattern matching maintains consistent state between pointer position (`val.data`) and remaining length (`val.length`), and that buffer accesses are validated against the length before dereferencing.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

