The fix changes the semantic definition of "in core" from "page is in the system-wide page cache" to "page is actually mapped in the calling process's address space." This eliminates the cross-process information leak through shared cache state.

**Change 1: Semantic Redefinition for Unmapped Pages**
<enforcement spec="HS-SEC-001">
<location>mm/mincore.c, mincore_unmapped_range()</location>
<diff>
 static int __mincore_unmapped_range(unsigned long addr, unsigned long end,
				struct vm_area_struct *vma, unsigned char *vec)
 {
	unsigned long nr = (end - addr) >> PAGE_SHIFT;
	int i;

	if (vma->vm_file) {
		pgoff_t pgoff;

		pgoff = linear_page_index(vma, addr);
		for (i = 0; i < nr; i++, pgoff++)
			vec[i] = mincore_page(vma->vm_file->f_mapping, pgoff);
	} else {
		for (i = 0; i < nr; i++)
			vec[i] = 0;
	}
	return nr;
 }

 static int mincore_unmapped_range(unsigned long addr, unsigned long end,
				   struct mm_walk *walk)
 {
	unsigned char *vec = walk->private;
	unsigned long nr = (end - addr) >> PAGE_SHIFT;

	memset(vec, 0, nr);
	walk->private += nr;
	return 0;
 }
</diff>
    <compliance_mechanism>
        The fixed version completely removes the call to `__mincore_unmapped_range()` and `mincore_page()`. Instead, it simply sets all bytes in the output vector to 0 for unmapped pages, regardless of whether those pages exist in the system-wide page cache. This ensures that mincore() only reports information about the calling process's actual memory mappings, not shared cache state.
    </compliance_mechanism>
</enforcement>

